<h1>Build a Gaming PC: The Key Parts</h1>
<p>These are the main components that decide whether your PC can run the latest games.</p>

<picture>
  <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/images/small.jpg.png">
  <source media="(max-width: 1000px)" srcset="/images/medium.jpg.png">
  <img src="/images/large.jpg.png" alt="Gaming PC setup" class="responsive-img">
</picture>

<div class="cards">
  <div class="card">
    <h2>Processor (CPU)</h2>
    <p>The brain of your computer. It handles game logic, physics and background tasks. More cores and higher clock speeds help in busy, complex games.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Graphics Card (GPU)</h2>
    <p>Draws everything you see on screen. It matters most for frame rate and resolution, and it is usually the first thing to check against a game's requirements.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Memory (RAM)</h2>
    <p>Short-term storage for the game while it runs. Too little RAM causes stuttering and slow loading. 16GB is a common recommendation for modern games.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Storage (SSD)</h2>
    <p>Where your games are installed. An SSD loads games much faster than a traditional hard drive, and modern titles often need 50GB or more of free space.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Power Supply (PSU)</h2>
    <p>Delivers power to every part. A powerful graphics card needs a PSU with enough wattage, or the PC can crash or shut down under load.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Cooling</h2>
    <p>Fans and heatsinks keep temperatures down. A hot CPU or GPU slows itself down, which lowers performance during long gaming sessions.</p>
  </div>
</div>